rgo.toml and src/main.rs exist; Cargo.toml [dependencies] is empty.\n\nStep 2 \u2014 Write the server:\nAfter Step 1, rewrite src/main.rs (bash-only) so the program binds port 8080 and serves the\ncanonical response in a loop. You write ALL of the code yourself \u2014 the notes below are\nrequirements and known failure modes, NOT source code to copy.\n\n  Scope \u2014 what to build and what to skip:\n  - Respond to ANY request identically. Do NOT build request parsing, URL routing, or\n    threading \u2014 the spec returns the same response to every request, so those are pure bug\n    surface. The whole program is: bind the port, an accept loop, and one fixed response.\n  - The only \"protocol\" you must construct by hand (no crates) is the HTTP response itself.\n\n  Requirements your response must satisfy (these are HTTP facts, not Rust API hints):\n  - It must be a well-formed HTTP/1.1 message: a status line, then header lines, then a\n    blank line, then the body. The body is the literal text `hello` followed by a newline.\n  - The lines in the HEADER section (status line, each header, and the blank line that\n    terminates the headers) must end with CRLF, not a bare newline \u2014 a bare newline there\n    makes curl reject the response.\n  - The client must be able to tell when the body has ended, or `curl -s` will hang waiting\n    for more: send a Content-Length header whose value equals the body's byte length, and/or\n    a Connection: close header (sending both is safest).\n\n  Loop & robustness requirements:\n  - Bind the port ONCE, before the accept loop \u2014 never inside it (re-binding fails with\n    \"address already in use\").\n  - The accept loop must never exit on its own, and must survive a single bad connection\n    (one client/IO error must not kill the server).\n  - When reading the incoming request, do NOT read it to end-of-stream. The client sends its\n    request and then waits for YOUR response \u2014 it never closes its side \u2014 so r